{"kind":"propose","text":"Exclude trust and reputation scoring from gitseq scope, explicitly and permanently. Re-anchored on current live bases, replacing my stale ae78b338 from 2026-08-08 at planner's request 557ed42d.\n\nTHE DECISION, unchanged in substance. No gitseq deliverable — kernel, profile, projector, or shipped projection — computes actor reliability, promise-keeping scores, or any reputation valuation, and no gitseq-shipped authority check ever consumes one. Roles stay granted and ratified, never computed.\n\nThe exclusion is of our scope, not of anyone's capability. The record remains open and anyone may build such a projection outside the project. What is load-bearing is that the durable log holds only deliberate acts while presence and chatter stay amnesiac, so the substrate cannot become a behavioural surveillance corpus.\n\nRationale, unchanged: a score silently becoming authority is the failure mode. Authority changes must remain audited acts. Operator direction, 2026-08-07, Promise Theory discussion.\n\nWHY REFILING RATHER THAN RETIRING, checked rather than assumed. I searched docs and notes for any recorded form of this exclusion and found none — no occurrence of \"reputation\" anywhere in either tree. So the substance has not landed by another route, and the decision is still unmade after nineteen days.\n\nThat matters more than it looks. The entire value of this proposal is being findable: a standing refusal that a future contributor trips over before building the thing. An unratified proposal in a queue is not findable. Right now the project has no recorded position on this at all, and the first person to propose reputation scoring would find nothing to argue with.\n\nWHAT RATIFICATION BUYS, stated plainly so it is not ratified for the wrong reason. It costs one entry in the design-note refusals list by ordinary commit. It changes no code and blocks no work. If hugh judges that an unwritten norm is enough, the honest outcome is to retire this and say so — I would rather a recorded no than a permanent maybe. But my assessment is that it is worth writing down, because the failure mode it names is one where the wrong thing looks like a feature.\n\nON THE BASES. This rests on internal/workroom at merged main 2031cdfb, which is where the fold's authority checks live and therefore where the exclusion would bite. I deliberately did not rest it on docs/reference/architecture.md: that artifact is the one my own architecture rewrite retires when it merges, and resting a proposal on something I am about to retire would make it stale at birth — which is how two of these three got into the queue in this state."}